{"id":3557,"date":"2018-10-12T17:46:42","date_gmt":"2018-10-12T21:46:42","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/?p=3557"},"modified":"2018-10-20T16:48:07","modified_gmt":"2018-10-20T20:48:07","slug":"bruges-belgium","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/travel-log\/bruges-belgium\/","title":{"rendered":"Bruges, Belgium"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Bruges is a picturesque little city of breweries, swans and chocolates. The city tells of a people who have continuously fought for independence and for a right to identity from various global powers over time. Once an important city for cloth trading, it is now a romantic city for tourists.<\/p>\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-3454 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-3454\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3903.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Markt is the central square of Bruges, with the Belfort on one side, the West Flanders Provincial Court on another and various restaurants on the other two sides.\n\n&lt;p&gt;At the center of the square, there is a statue of Pieter de Coninck and Jan Breydel. This statue is meant to represent northern Belgium&#039;s insistence on their Flemish identity. In 1302, Coninck, a weaver, and Breydel, a butcher, organized a revolt against the occupying Kingdom of France. The County of Flanders were the obvious winners, incurring only about 300 deaths, while the French suffered a loss of 1,500 men. This battle came to be known as the Battle of the Golden Spurs. The statue was put up in 1887 and is a reminder of what Flemish ancestors have done to preserve the Flemish identity.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3484\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Markt is the central square of Bruges, with the Belfort on one side, the West Flanders Provincial Court on another and various restaurants on the other two sides.\n\n&lt;p&gt;At the center of the square, there is a statue of Pieter de Coninck and Jan Breydel. This statue is meant to represent northern Belgium&#039;s insistence on their Flemish identity. In 1302, Coninck, a weaver, and Breydel, a butcher, organized a revolt against the occupying Kingdom of France. The County of Flanders were the obvious winners, incurring only about 300 deaths, while the French suffered a loss of 1,500 men. This battle came to be known as the Battle of the Golden Spurs. The statue was put up in 1887 and is a reminder of what Flemish ancestors have done to preserve the Flemish identity.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3903\/1319918302.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Markt is the central square of Bruges, with the Belfort on one side, the West Flanders Provincial Court on another and various restaurants on the other two sides.\n\n<p>At the center of the square, there is a statue of Pieter de Coninck and Jan Breydel. This statue is meant to represent northern Belgium's insistence on their Flemish identity. In 1302, Coninck, a weaver, and Breydel, a butcher, organized a revolt against the occupying Kingdom of France. The County of Flanders were the obvious winners, incurring only about 300 deaths, while the French suffered a loss of 1,500 men. This battle came to be known as the Battle of the Golden Spurs. The statue was put up in 1887 and is a reminder of what Flemish ancestors have done to preserve the Flemish identity.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3906.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Back in the Middle Ages, the market place was actually a rather large harbor. As a result, in 1294, a Waterhalle was built. Later, in 1787, the building was demolished and replaced with the neoclassicist building, housing the local government. Another hundred years later, that building was demolished and replaced with a neogothical building, intended to house the post office and the local government. That is the building that currently stands before us. However, instead of a post office, there is now Historium (on the left). Additionally, the central part of the building is no longer used for government meetings but instead only for ceremonial events.\" data-attachment-id=\"3485\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Back in the Middle Ages, the market place was actually a rather large harbor. As a result, in 1294, a Waterhalle was built. Later, in 1787, the building was demolished and replaced with the neoclassicist building, housing the local government. Another hundred years later, that building was demolished and replaced with a neogothical building, intended to house the post office and the local government. That is the building that currently stands before us. However, instead of a post office, there is now Historium (on the left). Additionally, the central part of the building is no longer used for government meetings but instead only for ceremonial events.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3906\/2744598258.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Back in the Middle Ages, the market place was actually a rather large harbor. As a result, in 1294, a Waterhalle was built. Later, in 1787, the building was demolished and replaced with the neoclassicist building, housing the local government. Another hundred years later, that building was demolished and replaced with a neogothical building, intended to house the post office and the local government. That is the building that currently stands before us. However, instead of a post office, there is now Historium (on the left). Additionally, the central part of the building is no longer used for government meetings but instead only for ceremonial events.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3902.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The Belfort (Dutch for Belfry) of Bruges was the city watchtower. Now it is simply a bell tower containing 47 bells that can play any song.\n\n&lt;p&gt;The Belfort was first built in 1240 out of wood. Forty years later, it caught fire and had to be rebuilt. Thankfully, it was rebuilt out of stone, almost to the top. Yet, because the architect wanted to make the building even taller and more imposing, a wooden spire was added to the top. Being the tallest building in Bruges, the spire fell victim to a lightning strike. The spire along with the bells were destroyed. The spire was rebuilt once more before being hit by lightning again, at which point, the spire was no longer rebuilt.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3483\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The Belfort (Dutch for Belfry) of Bruges was the city watchtower. Now it is simply a bell tower containing 47 bells that can play any song.\n\n&lt;p&gt;The Belfort was first built in 1240 out of wood. Forty years later, it caught fire and had to be rebuilt. Thankfully, it was rebuilt out of stone, almost to the top. Yet, because the architect wanted to make the building even taller and more imposing, a wooden spire was added to the top. Being the tallest building in Bruges, the spire fell victim to a lightning strike. The spire along with the bells were destroyed. The spire was rebuilt once more before being hit by lightning again, at which point, the spire was no longer rebuilt.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3902\/1306804538.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The Belfort (Dutch for Belfry) of Bruges was the city watchtower. Now it is simply a bell tower containing 47 bells that can play any song.\n\n<p>The Belfort was first built in 1240 out of wood. Forty years later, it caught fire and had to be rebuilt. Thankfully, it was rebuilt out of stone, almost to the top. Yet, because the architect wanted to make the building even taller and more imposing, a wooden spire was added to the top. Being the tallest building in Bruges, the spire fell victim to a lightning strike. The spire along with the bells were destroyed. The spire was rebuilt once more before being hit by lightning again, at which point, the spire was no longer rebuilt.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3829.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The rectangular building at the base of the Belfort was historically used as a marketplace. With the harbor right in front of the Belfort, it is logical that this is where the merchants came to sell their wares. During the medieval period, Bruges was a well-known city for textiles.\" data-attachment-id=\"3489\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The rectangular building at the base of the Belfort was historically used as a marketplace. With the harbor right in front of the Belfort, it is logical that this is where the merchants came to sell their wares. During the medieval period, Bruges was a well-known city for textiles.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3829\/1878101378.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The rectangular building at the base of the Belfort was historically used as a marketplace. With the harbor right in front of the Belfort, it is logical that this is where the merchants came to sell their wares. During the medieval period, Bruges was a well-known city for textiles.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3832.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This triumphal arch was placed in 1930 in memory of those who died during the First World War.  The flag of Burges is depicted next to the left pillar while the flag of Flanders is depicted next to the right pillar. At the top, on both sides, urns have been placed to symbolize the fallen. Finally, the arch is crowned by Belgium&#039;s coat of arms.\" data-attachment-id=\"3459\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This triumphal arch was placed in 1930 in memory of those who died during the First World War.  The flag of Burges is depicted next to the left pillar while the flag of Flanders is depicted next to the right pillar. At the top, on both sides, urns have been placed to symbolize the fallen. Finally, the arch is crowned by Belgium&#039;s coat of arms.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3832\/390676732.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This triumphal arch was placed in 1930 in memory of those who died during the First World War.  The flag of Burges is depicted next to the left pillar while the flag of Flanders is depicted next to the right pillar. At the top, on both sides, urns have been placed to symbolize the fallen. Finally, the arch is crowned by Belgium's coat of arms.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3835.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Belgian chocolatiers are among the most respected in the world. In order to determine whether a chocolate store is authentic, look for the oval chocolate bean plaque next to the entrance.\" data-attachment-id=\"3460\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Belgian chocolatiers are among the most respected in the world. In order to determine whether a chocolate store is authentic, look for the oval chocolate bean plaque next to the entrance.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3835\/524113266.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Belgian chocolatiers are among the most respected in the world. In order to determine whether a chocolate store is authentic, look for the oval chocolate bean plaque next to the entrance.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3837.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The mathematician who invented the decimal system for fractions, Simon Stevin, was born in Bruges in 1548 and now has a square named after him.\" data-attachment-id=\"3461\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The mathematician who invented the decimal system for fractions, Simon Stevin, was born in Bruges in 1548 and now has a square named after him.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3837\/4173543495.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The mathematician who invented the decimal system for fractions, Simon Stevin, was born in Bruges in 1548 and now has a square named after him.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3834.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This cute little square was named after him: Simon Stevinplein. It also features a statue of him (behind the trees).\" data-attachment-id=\"3559\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This cute little square was named after him: Simon Stevinplein. It also features a statue of him (behind the trees).\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3834\/1155637739.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This cute little square was named after him: Simon Stevinplein. It also features a statue of him (behind the trees).<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3843.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"In Belgium, the way that people would show off their wealth was by building a facade with the most amount of steps at the top. In this picture, the owners of the twin houses were wealthier than the owners of the house to the right. The house to the right though also shows a symbol of wealth by declaring that renovated the house in 1669.\" data-attachment-id=\"3462\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"In Belgium, the way that people would show off their wealth was by building a facade with the most amount of steps at the top. In this picture, the owners of the twin houses were wealthier than the owners of the house to the right. The house to the right though also shows a symbol of wealth by declaring that renovated the house in 1669.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3843\/771023853.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">In Belgium, the way that people would show off their wealth was by building a facade with the most amount of steps at the top. In this picture, the owners of the twin houses were wealthier than the owners of the house to the right. The house to the right though also shows a symbol of wealth by declaring that renovated the house in 1669.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3877.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Saint John\u2019s Hospital treated patients from the twelfth to the mid-nineteenth century. It is one of the oldest hospitals in Europe. The hospital was run by nuns, who unfortunately did not have much of a medical background and tended mostly to spiritual health instead.\" data-attachment-id=\"3475\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Saint John\u2019s Hospital treated patients from the twelfth to the mid-nineteenth century. It is one of the oldest hospitals in Europe. The hospital was run by nuns, who unfortunately did not have much of a medical background and tended mostly to spiritual health instead.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3877\/661903456.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Saint John\u2019s Hospital treated patients from the twelfth to the mid-nineteenth century. It is one of the oldest hospitals in Europe. The hospital was run by nuns, who unfortunately did not have much of a medical background and tended mostly to spiritual health instead.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3855.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"In 2015, the oldest brewery in Bruges--De Halve Maan--started to work on its beer pipeline project. Because the company&#039;s bottling plant is located outside of the city, De Halve Maan (i.e. The Half Moon) wanted to find a way to transport the beer from the brewery to the bottling plant without relying on tankers. Their solution was going underground.\n&lt;p&gt;The project was completed in September 2016 and is officially the first beer pipeline in the world. The three kilometer pipeline cost 4 million euros, 300 thousand of which were raised through crowd-sourcing. The largest contribution level in this crowd-sourcing program was 7,500 euros, the gold level. Those who subscribed to this gold membership were given 18 personalized glasses as well as a beer a day for the rest of their life. A great marketing scheme, if you ask me!&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"3465\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"In 2015, the oldest brewery in Bruges--De Halve Maan--started to work on its beer pipeline project. Because the company&#039;s bottling plant is located outside of the city, De Halve Maan (i.e. The Half Moon) wanted to find a way to transport the beer from the brewery to the bottling plant without relying on tankers. Their solution was going underground.\n&lt;p&gt;The project was completed in September 2016 and is officially the first beer pipeline in the world. The three kilometer pipeline cost 4 million euros, 300 thousand of which were raised through crowd-sourcing. The largest contribution level in this crowd-sourcing program was 7,500 euros, the gold level. Those who subscribed to this gold membership were given 18 personalized glasses as well as a beer a day for the rest of their life. A great marketing scheme, if you ask me!&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2018\/10\/DSC_3855\/855827795.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">In 2015, the oldest brewery in Bruges--De Halve Maan--started to work on its beer pipeline project. Because the company's bottling plant is located outside of the city, De Halve Maan (i.e. The Half Moon) wanted to find a way to transport the beer from the brewery to the bottling plant without relying on tankers. Their solution was going underground.\n<p>The project was completed in September 2016 and is officially the first beer pipeline in the world. The three kilometer pipeline cost 4 million euros, 300 thousand of which were raised through crowd-sourcing. The largest contribution level in this crowd-sourcing program was 7,500 euros, the gold level. Those who subscribed to this gold membership were given 18 personalized glasses as well as a beer a day for the rest of their life. A great marketing scheme, if you ask me!<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2018\/10\/DSC_3857.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Bruges is known for its hand-lacing industry. This is the machine used in the process.\" data-attachment-id=\"3466\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Bruges is known for its hand-lacing industry. This is the machine used in the process.\"